<section class="wrap band">
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="head"><p class="eyebrow">What to do now</p><h2>Four moves, in order.</h2></div>
    <div class="steps"><div><p class="n">01</p><h4>Know what you have signed</h4><p>If you granted an option, conditional contract, pre-emption or promotion agreement since 8 June 2026, the developer must report it. Ask them to confirm when they have.</p></div><div><p class="n">02</p><h4>Read the neighbourhood</h4><p>From April 2028 search the dataset by location. An adjoining field under a seven-year option tells you who will knock on your door, and when.</p></div><div><p class="n">03</p><h4>Use the expiry</h4><p>A right that lapses with no planning submitted is a negotiating moment. The developer knows it. Now you do too.</p></div><div><p class="n">04</p><h4>Take advice before you sign</h4><p>Visibility cuts both ways. A promoter approaching you can see every other agreement in the area. Get a land agent or solicitor who can too.</p></div></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="wrap band">
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="body"><h3>What the regulations do for you</h3>
<p>Parliament built this regime because contractual controls were invisible to landowners and communities. The stated aim is that a landowner entering an agreement with a developer or promoter has information about agreements over other land in their area. From April 2028, that is exactly what the dataset provides: location and extent of land, the grantee, the type of right, its duration, and the dates it was granted or exercised.</p>
<h3>What you will not see</h3>
<p>The price. Option fees, purchase formulas and promotion splits are not reported. Nor are agreements over unregistered land, overage and clawback deals, or agreements signed before 8 June 2026 that are never varied, assigned or exercised afterwards.</p>
<h3>What is reported about you</h3>
<p>Where the grantor is an individual rather than a company, HM Land Registry guidance indicates that date and place of birth details may be required for identification. How personal details are treated in the published dataset is for HMLR’s forthcoming practice guide; company identifiers are published, personal identifiers are handled separately. Ask your solicitor to confirm the position before the agreement is reported.</p>
<h3>Negotiating in the open</h3>
<p>Law firms advising developers are already warning them that adjoining owners will take a firmer stance on ransom strips, access and strategic parcels once agreements are visible. If you own one of those parcels, the dataset is the first place to look before any conversation about price.</p>
<h3>If you are approached now</h3>
<p>Anything you grant today is caught and will be public from 2028. Ask the developer which conveyancer will report it, confirm the control period and extension terms in plain English, and ask what happens at expiry. A good land agent will already be reading the register for you by the time it exists.</p></div>
